course: dictionary
lesson: 8261
title: Get rid of: meaning in phrases
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rid is a short word with a big job, and it almost always comes in the phrase get rid of. «The 24-hour clock gets rid of am and pm» means the clock no longer uses am and pm. Read the words around rid and they will tell you what it means.
explain: Rid can also mean to clear a place of something. Think of the hero in a story who rid the town of the dragon: he cleared the dragon out of the town. Try the swap test: put "removed" or "cleared out" in place of rid, and check the sentence still makes sense.
explain: To be rid of something means you are free of it at last, like the moment a cold has finally gone. Get rid of is the phrase to remember, because it means to throw away, remove or make something go away.
fact: Rid is nearly always used in the phrase get rid of.
fact: «Get rid of» means to throw away, remove or make something go away.
fact: To rid a place of something means to clear it out.
fact: «Washing towels at 60°C gets rid of more germs.»
fact: To be rid of something means to be free of it at last.
exercise: «The 24-hour clock gets rid of am and pm.» What does gets rid of mean here? | Adds more of, so there are more am and pm | *Counts them one by one | Removes, so am and pm are no longer used | Hides them for a while
exercise: In «Washing towels at 60°C gets rid of more germs», which word could replace gets rid of? | dries | *removes | spreads | shares
exercise: «Redundancy means the job itself is being got rid of, not that the person did anything wrong.» What does this suggest? | The person was naughty and was sacked | The job is getting bigger | The person won a prize | *The job is being removed, not the person's fault
test: «Burps, farts, snot, sneezes, hiccups and poo make everyone giggle, but each one is the body doing a useful job: getting rid of waste.» What does getting rid of waste mean? | Eating more waste | Making waste bigger | *Removing waste from the body | Storing waste inside the body
test: What does it mean to be rid of a cold? | Catching it again | Feeling it getting worse | *Being free of it at last | Sharing it with a friend
exam: Which phrase is rid almost always used in? | *get rid of | sit down | go on | in the end
